          "text": "# Bug Nebula: A Dying Star\u2019s Last, Luminous Gesture\n\n*NGC 6302 is a planetary nebula in the southern sky. Its name suggests an insect; its science tells a story of a star shedding its outer layers.*\n\nThe Bug Nebula sounds as if it ought to be darting across the sky. Instead, NGC 6302 holds its place in Scorpius, a cloud of glowing gas around the remains of a star. \u201cBug\u201d is a nickname, not a classification: the astronomical catalog SIMBAD identifies NGC 6302 as a planetary nebula. The word *planetary* is a historical label, too. These objects are not planets, but expanding shells of material associated with stars late in their lives. [SIMBAD: NGC 6302](https://simbad.cds.unistra.fr/simbad/sim-id?Ident=NGC%206302)\n\nA planetary nebula marks a transition. After a star has spent much of its life producing energy in its core, it can cast off its outer gas. The exposed stellar remnant then floods that material with energetic light, making the gas glow. The nebula is not a static ornament: it is a brief, changing phase in the life cycle of a star. NGC 6302\u2019s catalog entry places it in this broad family; the nickname gives the eye a way into its more intricate appearance.\n\nThe coordinates offer a practical route to it: right ascension 17 hours 13 minutes 44.5 seconds, declination 37 degrees 6 minutes 11 seconds south, for the J2000 reference frame. In the radio data listed by SIMBAD, its angular dimensions are about 0.47 by 0.45 arcminutes\u2014roughly 28 by 27 arcseconds. That measurement is for radio observations, so it should not be treated as a precise description of what an eyepiece will show. [SIMBAD: coordinates and listed angular size](https://simbad.cds.unistra.fr/simbad/sim-id?Ident=NGC%206302)\n\nFor observers, the declination is a useful warning as well as a locator. The nebula lies far south of the celestial equator. It climbs higher for observers in the Southern Hemisphere; from northern latitudes it stays comparatively low, where atmospheric haze can soften the view. Plan to look when Scorpius is well placed, and choose a site with a clear southern horizon. A planetarium app or telescope\u2019s go-to system can turn the coordinates into a finding aid; a printed chart can help confirm the surrounding star field.\n\nDo not expect the Bug Nebula to announce itself to the unaided eye. Use a telescope, begin with a low-power eyepiece to locate the field, then try more magnification if the image remains sharp. Give your eyes time to adapt to darkness, and avoid judging the view through a bright phone screen. The nebula\u2019s listed radio size suggests a compact target, but actual visibility depends on aperture, sky conditions, magnification and the observer. If it looks faint or small, that is not failure: planetary nebulae can be subtle sights, especially when low above the horizon.\n\nThe name asks us to see an insect; the telescope invites a slower kind of attention. Somewhere inside that patch of sky, a star has released gas that now shines under the influence of its remnant. What looks, at first, like a tiny mark is a stage in a stellar ending\u2014and a reminder that the sky\u2019s most delicate shapes can come from immense changes."
